# CSS architecture

## Ownership and organization

- Component-specific styles should live next to the component they belong to.
- Prefer a component directory containing its TSX file and associated styles.
- Keep global styles limited to genuine application-wide concerns:
  resets, base typography, design tokens, global themes, and document-level styling.
- Keep page-specific styles with the page when they are not shared elsewhere.
- Shared styling primitives belong in a clearly named shared styles or design-system area.

## Encapsulation

- Prefer CSS Modules for component-specific styles when they improve isolation
  and reduce collisions with existing global selectors.
- Use .module.css only when supported by the existing build configuration.
- Do not convert all CSS to CSS Modules blindly.
- Identify global selectors, shared class names, CSS variables, and cross-component
  dependencies before migrating styles.
- Avoid styling unrelated components through broad element selectors or
  undocumented parent-child dependencies.
- Preserve responsive behavior, states, animations, themes, and visual appearance.

## Migration

- Inventory existing CSS files and their imports before moving styles.
- Identify which components actually use each selector.
- Migrate one logical group at a time.
- Update imports and class references together.
- Remove old styles only after verifying that they are no longer needed.
- Avoid changing layout, spacing, colors, or typography as part of a purely structural migration.
- Compare the application before and after the migration and investigate visual regressions.
